  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nOn Sunday and Monday the southwestern U.S. 500 MB trough \n(discussed in the short term section above) will finally drift \nslowly northeastward toward the central U.S. Even as this \noccurs, a stronger cyclone will come ashore over the U.S. \nPacific Northwest coast. The leading trough will gradually wash \nout as it moves over Iowa sometime around Monday night or \nTuesday, but the details of that evolution remain fuzzy at long \nrange. Have maintained rain chances Monday into Tuesday across \nmost of our area, but it remains to be seen how far in from the \nnorthwest the surface trough/rain will be able to penetrate \nduring this time. We are likely to see a sharp temperature \ngradient across Iowa on Sunday, with the far northwest being \ncooler in clouds and showers where temperatures will probably \npeak in the 50s, while in the south and east sunny and warm \nweather will persist for another day with highs well into the \n70s. This may also occur to some extent on Monday, but \nconfidence in temperatures is low that day due to the \nuncertainties in location/timing of the surface trough, clouds, \nand rain. Once the leading 500 MB trough passes, there will be a\nbrief respite with weak mid-level ridging before the stronger \ntrough from the Pacific Northwest swings through our region in \nthe middle of next week. That system will push a stronger \nsurface trough through and sweep out most remaining moisture \nfrom the earlier systems, bringing quieter autumn weather for at\nleast a couple of days afterward.",
